WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.038
Picture a busy café.

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:08.118
On a whiteboard by the till, the owner keeps today's takings.

3
00:00:08.118 --> 00:00:10.192
Each sale, she adds the amount.

4
00:00:10.192 --> 00:00:12.199
Each refund, she takes it off.

5
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:17.536
When a regular asks how the day is going, she reads the board instead of counting the till.

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:22.990
One afternoon she adds a second line: cheapest sale today, 1.20, a small tea.

7
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.472
Then the customer brings the tea back for a refund.

8
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.074
The takings are easy: take off 1.20.

9
00:00:29.074 --> 00:00:31.786
But what should the second line say now?

10
00:00:31.786 --> 00:00:33.075
Hold that question.

11
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.598
A database can keep the same board.

12
00:00:35.598 --> 00:00:40.652
The receipts are a table of payments, in PostgreSQL, a free database program.

13
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:48.885
The board is a stored answer to a question people keep asking: for each shop, how many payments, their total, and the smallest.

14
00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:52.421
A stored answer like that is called a materialized view.

15
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.119
The plain way to keep it right is a refresh: work the whole answer out again.

16
00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.933
In the lesson, that read a million payments to change one number.

17
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.957
It's counting the whole till after every sale.

18
00:01:03.957 --> 00:01:07.684
What we want is to change only what the new payment changes.

19
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.424
Let's shrink it.

20
00:01:09.424 --> 00:01:11.754
Four payments, two shops.

21
00:01:11.754 --> 00:01:18.556
Shop M-0042 has 3 payments, 10,450 cents in all, and the smallest is 300.

22
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:23.071
Now a payment arrives: shop M-0042, 150.

23
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.988
Watch the view.

24
00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:26.564
Count, plus 1.

25
00:01:26.564 --> 00:01:28.139
Sum, plus 150.

26
00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:33.204
Minimum, the smaller of 300 and 150: now 150.

27
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:36.910
All of it came from the new payment and the old view row.

28
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.043
Not one row of the table was read.

29
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:46.035
A change to the input is called a delta, and the rule that turns it into a change to the answer is a delta rule.

30
00:01:46.035 --> 00:01:49.679
It's the owner adding each sale to the board as it rings up.

31
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:53.099
Now a refund deletes the 8,900.

32
00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:58.715
Deletes mirror inserts: count minus 1, sum minus 8,900.

33
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.980
And 8,900 was above the minimum, so the minimum can't have changed.

34
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.420
Still nothing read.

35
00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.146
So far so good.

36
00:02:07.146 --> 00:02:10.104
Now delete payment 9, the 150.

37
00:02:10.104 --> 00:02:12.175
That was the minimum.

38
00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:16.317
Count and sum are easy: 2 payments, 1,550.

39
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.131
What's the new minimum?

40
00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:19.265
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.984
You can see it's 300, because the table is on screen.

42
00:02:25.984 --> 00:02:26.970
The view can't.

43
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.373
It kept one number per shop, and the next smallest was never in it.

44
00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.125
That's the refunded tea.

45
00:02:33.125 --> 00:02:37.084
The board said 1.20, and nothing on it says what came second.

46
00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:38.823
There are two ways out.

47
00:02:38.823 --> 00:02:42.052
One: go back to the receipts, but only this shop's.

48
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:52.044
Through an index on the shop, a lookup that finds rows by shop, the view reads M-0042's two remaining payments and takes the smaller: 300.

49
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:55.808
That costs one shop's rows, not the whole table.

50
00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:59.457
pg_ivm, an add-on for PostgreSQL, works this way.

51
00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:01.334
The other way: store more.

52
00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:07.296
Keep every amount, sorted, for every shop, like a tray of receipts in order beside the board.

53
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.949
The next smallest is always at hand, and you pay in memory instead.

54
00:03:11.949 --> 00:03:17.327
Streaming databases, which keep answers updated as data flows in, take that road.

55
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.928
Last change: delete shop M-0007's only payment.

56
00:03:20.928 --> 00:03:24.397
Its count drops to 0, so its row leaves the view.

57
00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:28.496
Four changes cost 2 table rows read and 4 view rows written.

58
00:03:28.496 --> 00:03:32.687
A refresh after each would have read the whole table four times.

59
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:38.911
Keeping a stored answer right one change at a time is called incremental view maintenance.

60
00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:42.089
So should every view work by deltas?

61
00:03:42.089 --> 00:03:42.982
Not always.

62
00:03:42.982 --> 00:03:54.589
In the lesson's lab, 1,000 small changes to 20,000 payments cost 1,402 rows of work as deltas, against 20 million for a refresh after each one.

63
00:03:54.767 --> 00:04:06.080
But a batch that deleted 90% of the table cost 35,994 rows as deltas, against 2,200 for one refresh at the end.

64
00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:10.640
Most of it went on rescanning shops whose minimum had just been deleted.

65
00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.705
Deltas are good with small change.

66
00:04:12.705 --> 00:04:16.228
When a batch rewrites most of the data, work it out again.

67
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.554
So, what should the second line say?

68
00:04:18.554 --> 00:04:20.951
The board can't tell without the receipts.

69
00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:25.424
Counts and sums can be added and taken back, one change at a time.

70
00:04:25.424 --> 00:04:30.908
The smallest can be added to, but not taken back, unless you look again or kept more.

71
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:32.710
One quick check.

72
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:37.436
Shop M-0042 has two payments left, 1,250 and 300.

73
00:04:37.436 --> 00:04:40.040
A refund deletes the 1,250.

74
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.609
Does the view need to read the table?

75
00:04:43.609 --> 00:04:45.152
Pause and think.

76
00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:48.593
No.

77
00:04:48.593 --> 00:04:53.175
1,250 is above the minimum, so the minimum stays 300.

78
00:04:53.175 --> 00:04:58.103
Only deleting the minimum sends you back to the receipts.
